General 4-point argument (holds in any finite-dim GPT): an orbit of a compact connected drift through a moved point
is a closed curve around its centroid c; points at phases 0,pi and pi/2,3pi/2 average to c, so
T(c) = (p0p0+p2p2)/2 = (p1p1+p3p3)/2 is required; a product effect f(x)f separates them whenever f's slope differs on the two diameters.
   disk: (f(x)f) on clone-of-diameter-1 = 0.500, on clone-of-diameter-2 = 0.250  -> contradiction: True

Dimension count: smallest state space with (i) finite capacity N, (ii) a continuous reversible drift moving some pure state,
(iii) N jointly distinguishable pure states fixed by the drift (a copyable record that survives the drift).
Claim [PROVED-TOY]: affine dimension d >= N+1. Table:
   classical N-simplex    d=N-1    fails (no continuous symmetry)
   disk/rebit, N=2        d=2      fails (SO(2) fixes only the centre)
   ball/qubit, N=2        d=3      meets d=N+1 with equality
   double cone, N=2       d=3      meets (apexes fixed)
   quantum, N levels      d=N^2-1  >= N+1, equality only N=2

Quantum check: dimension of the connected drift group that fixes two basis states and acts nontrivially (Lie algebra count)
   real QM, N=2: dim = 0
   real QM, N=3: dim = 0
   real QM, N=4: dim = 1
   complex QM, N=2: dim = 1
   complex QM, N=3: dim = 2
   complex QM, N=4: dim = 5
